<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aud the Deep-Minded</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Auðr djúpúðga Ketilsdóttir, Unn, Unnur Ketilsdottir, Aud Ketilsdatter</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aud the Deep-Minded was a 9th-century settler of Iceland, the daughter of the Norwegian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hersir">hersir</a> Ketill Flatnose and wife of Olaf the White, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>. After her son Thorstein the Red was killed in battle in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, she had a knarr built in secret at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caithness">Caithness</a> and sailed with her kin by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroes</a> to Breiðafjörður in Iceland, where she claimed land in Dalasýsla and freed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrall">thralls</a> aboard, giving them land to farm. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landnamabok">Landnámabók</a> describes her as a baptised Christian who raised crosses at Krosshólar, while Laxdæla saga, which calls her Unn, makes her a heathen given a ship burial. Many medieval Icelanders, among them the Sturlungs, descended from her.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Freedom often came with land to farm: <b>Aud the Deep-Minded</b>, who settled in Iceland in the late 9th century, freed the thralls she brought with her and gave them land, according to Landnámabók.</blockquote><a href="/societies/vikings">Social Hierarchy of the Vikings</a></li></ul></section>
